+
+# Decision: Cache Eviction Policy — 2Q
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker maintains a private 12 GiB cache of rendered JPEG contact sheets. Workloads alternate between long sequential screenings (many one-use sheets) and comparison sessions (repeated access to a small working set). The eviction policy must protect comparison sheets during screenings without adding bookkeeping that leaks outside the worker. Three candidates were evaluated.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **G1: Cache privacy** — All replacement metadata and file paths must stay private to the preview worker. Other nodes do not access cache files, pin entries, or inspect counters. (Inherited from HLD tenet: "Keep cache paths and replacement bookkeeping private to the preview worker.")
+- **G2: External interface stability** — The `GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}` contract (200 JPEG, 202 ticket, 404 unknown) must not change. (Inherited from previews LLD Architecture.)
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+| Criterion | Importance | Tenet |
+|-----------|------------|-------|
+| C1: Protects reused sheets from sequential-scan eviction | **major** | "Never treat a cache as the authoritative copy of source footage" — comparison sheets are the working set; evicting them defeats their purpose |
+| C2: Low bookkeeping overhead inside worker | **moderate** | — |
+| C3: Fast adaptation when editors switch projects | **moderate** | — |
+| C4: Implementation stays within worker (no cascade) | **minor** | — |
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Option A: LRU (Least Recently Used)
+
+**Description** — Single queue ordered by last access. On hit, move to head. On admission when over budget, evict from tail. One timestamp or position per entry.
+
+**Demonstration** — `cache.get(key)` moves entry to head; `cache.admit(entry)` evicts tail until under 12 GiB.
+
+**Analysis**
+- G1: **passes** — single queue, private to worker
+- G2: **passes** — no interface change
+- C1: **weak** — a long screening scan pushes all comparison sheets to tail and evicts them
+- C2: **strong** — minimal bookkeeping (one position/timestamp)
+- C3: **strong** — immediately follows recent work; project switch naturally evicts old project
+- C4: **strong** — single data structure
+
+**Summary** — Simple, fast adaptation, but sacrifices the comparison working set during scans.
+
+---
+
+### Option B: Frequency-Based Eviction with Aging
+
+**Description** — Each entry tracks an access counter. Periodically (or on admission), all counters are decayed (e.g., halved). On admission when over budget, evict lowest-counted entries. Requires counter per entry and aging sweep.
+
+**Demonstration** — `entry.count++` on hit; `age_all_counters()` periodically; `evict_lowest_count()` on pressure.
+
+**Analysis**
+- G1: **passes** — counters private to worker
+- G2: **passes** — no interface change
+- C1: **strong** — repeatedly accessed sheets accumulate high counts and resist eviction
+- C2: **weak** — counter per entry + periodic aging sweep + tie-breaking logic
+- C3: **weak** — aging lag keeps yesterday's favorites warm; project switch retains stale counts
+- C4: **moderate** — aging sweep adds background work
+
+**Summary** — Protects popular clips well, but counter maintenance and aging lag project switches.
+
+---
+
+### Option C: 2Q (Two-Queue)
+
+**Description** — Two LRU queues sharing the 12 GiB budget: A1in (first admission, 25% / 3 GiB target) and Am (promoted on second access, 75% / 9 GiB target). On first admission, entry goes to A1in head. On hit in A1in, promote to Am head. On hit in Am, move to Am head. On admission pressure: evict A1in tail first; when A1in ≤ 3 GiB, evict Am tail.
+
+**Demonstration** — `cache.admit(e)` → `A1in.push_head(e)`; `cache.get(k)` in A1in → `A1in.remove(e); Am.push_head(e)`; `cache.get(k)` in Am → `Am.move_to_head(e)`.
+
+**Analysis**
+- G1: **passes** — two queues, private to worker
+- G2: **passes** — no interface change
+- C1: **strong** — comparison sheets reach Am on second access; A1in absorbs scan traffic
+- C2: **moderate** — two queues + promotion logic + size tracking per queue
+- C3: **moderate** — project switch flushes A1in quickly; Am retains cross-project hot sheets (tunable via split)
+- C4: **moderate** — extra queue and promotion path, but no background work
+
+**Summary** — Scan-resistant by design; accepts extra bookkeeping to protect the comparison working set.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: Option C — 2Q.**
+
+It is the only option that passes G1/G2 and achieves **strong** on C1 (the major criterion tied to the "never treat cache as authoritative" tenet) while keeping C2/C3 at **moderate** rather than weak. The 25/75 split is the initial tuning parameter; it can be adjusted based on observed workload without changing the external interface or cascading to other segments.
+
+What this commits us to:
+- Two-queue implementation with promotion logic inside the preview worker
+- Per-queue byte-size tracking (not entry count) to honor the 3/9 GiB targets
+- The 25/75 split as a runtime-configurable parameter (hot-reloadable or restart-required — to be decided in implementation)
+- No background aging sweep — eviction happens on admission pressure only
\ No newline at end of file
